{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And he encountered the place.</b> Scripture did not specify which place, but said 'the place' — the place mentioned elsewhere: Mount Moriah, of which it is said concerning it: ״וַיַּרְא אֶת הַמָּקוֹם מֵרָחֹק״." "<b>And he encountered.</b> As in ״וּפָגַע בִּירִיחוֹ״ and ״וּפָגַע בְּדַבָּשֶׁת״ (Joshua 16, 19). And our Sages explained it as a term of prayer (Berakhot 26), as in ״וְאַל תִּפְגַּע בִּי״ (Jeremiah 7), and we learn from this that he established the evening prayer. And Scripture changed [the wording] and did not write 'and he prayed', to teach you that the earth contracted for him, as is explained in the chapter Gid ha-Nasheh (Chulin 91)." "<b>For the sun had set.</b> It should have written 'and the sun set and he lodged there'; 'for the sun had set' implies that the sun set for him suddenly, not at its proper time, so that he would lodge there." "<b>And he placed at his head.</b> He arranged them like a gutter around his head, for he feared wild beasts. They began quarreling with one another — this one said: 'Upon me the righteous one shall lay his head,' and that one said: 'Upon me.' Immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, made them one stone, and that is what is said: ״וַיִּקַּח אֶת הָאֶבֶן אֲשֶׁר שָׂם מְרַאֲשֹׁתָיו״." "<b>And he lay down in that place.</b> A term of restriction: in that place he lay down, but the fourteen years that he served in the house of Ever he did not lie down at night, for he was occupied with Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:01:00.071576427Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}